 ### [Hybrid Ray Tracing](2308-hybrid-ray-tracing.md)
-Hybrid Ray Tracing is a method for mixing geometric and volumetric data by casting rays through the ensemble of both, drawing samples of each, and compositing them in depth-sorted order. It solves the problem of rendering scenes containing both scalar fields (volumes) and polygonal surfaces simultaneously, ensuring correct visibility where polygons obscure volume and vice versa. Unlike [Texture-Based Volume Rendering](4707-texture-based-volume-rendering.md) which rasterizes proxy geometry, or [Mixed Geometry Rendering](3442-mixed-geometry-rendering.md) which may use hardware acceleration for static views, Hybrid Ray Tracing performs a unified ray-casting pass that explicitly handles the interaction between discrete polygonal intersections and continuous volumetric attenuation.
+Hybrid Ray Tracing is a method for mixing geometric and volumetric data by casting rays through the ensemble of both, drawing samples of each, and compositing them in depth-sorted order. It solves the problem of rendering scenes containing both scalar fields (volumes) and polygonal models simultaneously, ensuring correct visibility and occlusion between the two data types. Unlike pure volume rendering which ignores geometry, or pure polygon rendering which ignores volume density, this method treats polygons as embedded surfaces within the volumetric gel, handling the "hidden-volume" problem locally to avoid the expense of exact intersection tests.
 
