-#### Ray-to-Gravity-Center (R-GC) Volume Slicing
-- **Definition**: A volume slicing strategy where parallel cutting planes are oriented perpendicular to the straight line connecting the point of reflection to the gravity center of the volume domain, used to generate Layered Polygonal Area Lights (LPALs) [S4].
-- **Number**: Visual result: R-RR fails to accurately render the upper part of the reflected appearance when apart from the volume, whereas R-GC handles this correctly [S4].
-- **Comparison**: Compared to Ray-of-Regular-Reflection (R-RR) slicing: R-RR fails to capture the upper part of reflected appearance when the R-RR is apart from the volume because LPALs face away from the reflection point. R-GC ensures LPALs face properly to the point of reflection regardless of the regular reflection direction [S4].
+### Ray-to-Gravity-Center (R-GC) Volume Slicing
+- **Definition**: A volume slicing strategy where parallel cutting planes are oriented perpendicular to the straight line connecting the point of reflection to the gravity center of the volume domain, used to generate Layered Polygonal Area Lights (LPALs) [S3].
+- **Number**: Visual result: R-RR fails to accurately render the upper part of the reflected appearance when apart from the volume, whereas R-GC handles this correctly [S3].
+- **Comparison**: Compared to Ray-of-Regular-Reflection (R-RR) slicing: R-RR fails to capture the upper part of reflected appearance when the R-RR is apart from the volume because LPALs face away from the reflection point. R-GC ensures LPALs face properly to the point of reflection regardless of the regular reflection direction [S3].
 
 ## Background
-The technique originated with the advent of 3D texture hardware in the late 1990s and early 2000s. Early implementations, such as those by Krüger and Westermann (2003), utilized proxy geometry to leverage the graphics pipeline's texture sampling capabilities [S2, unofficial]. The "Object-Aligned Slicing" variant was popularized by Lefohn et al. (2004) and Kniss et al. (2002b), who addressed memory and sampling issues by replicating data along principal axes [S1]. View-aligned slicing was introduced to mitigate the sampling distance variations inherent in object-aligned approaches [S2, unofficial]. The method has been extended to Adaptive Mesh Refinement (AMR) data by Kähler and Hege (2001/2002) and Kähler et al. (2002), who applied it to astrophysical datasets like 'The First Star of the Universe' [S3, unofficial]. More recently, it has been adapted for indirect illumination via Layered Polygonal Area Lights (LPALs) using Ray-to-Gravity-Center slicing [S4].
+The technique was popularized in the early 2000s as graphics hardware evolved to support 3D textures and hardware alpha blending. Key early works include Lefohn et al. (2004) for on-the-fly slice reconstruction, Rezk-Salama et al. (2000) for trilinear interpolation in fragment shaders, and Kniss et al. (2002b) for addressing intensity variations and popping artifacts. The method is widely used in medical imaging and scientific visualization where interactive frame rates are required for large datasets.
 
